      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "A benzimidazole-containing compound reached 13 µM in rat brain after IP dosing, demonstrating that this benzimidazole scaffold can be brain penetrant when it lacks an ionizable carboxylic acid.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "A piperidine-phenethyl amide analogue showed a brain Cmax of 234 ng/g in rat, supporting CNS penetration for the piperidine-phenethyl motif, but this analogue is neutral and lacks the query's carboxylic acid.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "A related piperidine-phenethyl amide showed a brain/blood ratio of 2.3, again supporting the scaffold's CNS permeability only in the absence of a carboxylic acid.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The benzimidazole-piperidine scaffold is well represented by CNS-permeable analogues, but the query contains a carboxylic acid that would be largely ionized at physiological pH and form a zwitterion with the basic piperidine. No experimental record addresses an acid-containing analogue, and the strong passive-permeability evidence from neutral/basic analogues does not transfer to this charged species. Meaningful CNS access is therefore unlikely.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain/plasma, CSF, or uptake data for the exact query molecule or a close carboxylic acid analogue.",
    "No evidence on possible transporter-mediated CNS uptake (e.g., OATP, LAT1, MCT) for this specific zwitterionic acid.",
